From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PM / devfreq: Leave a suspended device's frequency for its resume

A min_freq or max_freq written while a device is suspended reaches the
driver at once: the QoS notifier reevaluates the target, and the
driver's target() sets the clock of a block whose power domain may be
off. On the RK3528, lima's GPU clock set to 800 MHz this way, with its
domain down, resets the SoC.

devfreq_suspend_device() already parks the device at its suspend
frequency, and devfreq_resume_device() restores it and resumes the
governor, which reevaluates the target. Leave the frequency alone in
between.

Signed-off-by: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c b/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c
index 82dd9a4..e06e2dd 100644
--- a/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c
+++ b/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c
@@ -408,6 +408,10 @@ int devfreq_update_target(struct devfreq *devfreq, unsigned long freq)
 	if (!devfreq->governor)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/* A suspended device is not clocked: its resume reevaluates the target */
+	if (atomic_read(&devfreq->suspend_count))
+		return 0;
+
 	/* Reevaluate the proper frequency */
 	err = devfreq->governor->get_target_freq(devfreq, &freq);
 	if (err)
